WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.540
So let me give you a really, really simple
example of a differentially private technique

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:07.680
for one task.

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:15.210
So suppose you have a big lecture hall filled
with 500 students or something, and you want

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:25.480
to know what fraction of them cheated on at
least one exam during their first year.

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:32.320
So you want to ask them about a behavior that's
not only embarrassing but probably a violation

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:34.830
of honor code.

00:00:34.830 --> 00:00:41.850
And you could give them the following instructions
— it's, you know, going to give them a method

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:44.230
of plausible deniability, basically.

00:00:44.230 --> 00:00:50.350
So the instructions are as follows: First,
flip a coin.

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:53.570
Don't tell me the outcome of the coin.

00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:58.290
If the coin came up heads, answer the question
truthfully.

00:00:58.290 --> 00:01:05.469
If the coin came up tails, flip another coin,
and if the second coin came up heads, say

00:01:05.469 --> 00:01:09.180
yes and otherwise say no.

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:16.120
So you see that a student who never cheated
still has a one-quarter probability of saying, "I cheated."

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:24.100
And a student who cheated still has a one-quarter
probability of saying, "I didn't cheat."

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:33.909
And so you can gather up now these basically
randomized responses, but randomized with

00:01:33.909 --> 00:01:35.789
a bias toward the truth.

00:01:35.789 --> 00:01:40.860
Randomized responses to your query and you
do a little bit of reverse engineering and

00:01:40.869 --> 00:01:45.799
you can get a close approximation to the true
fraction of students who cheated during — at

00:01:45.799 --> 00:01:47.790
some point during their first year.

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:51.979
Assuming the follow the instructions correctly.

00:01:51.979 --> 00:01:58.299
And that's called randomized response and
it's a technique that was developed in 1965

00:01:58.299 --> 00:02:02.729
by Stanley Warner.

00:02:02.729 --> 00:02:07.909
And differential privacy actually assigns
a formal measure of privacy lost there.

00:02:07.909 --> 00:02:16.879
That's associated with the ratio of the probabilities
of producing, say, the answer of 'Yes' when

00:02:16.879 --> 00:02:20.090
the truth is 'Yes,' versus when the truth
is 'No.'

00:02:20.090 --> 00:02:24.569
And it turns out that differential privacy
will tell you this is the logarithm of three

00:02:24.569 --> 00:02:26.090
differentially private.

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:28.340
That's our epsilon for that procedure.

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:35.180
You could make it more privacy protective
by increasing the chances that they will answer

00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:40.060
randomly and decreasing the chances that they
answer truthfully by instead of staring with

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:47.349
a fair coin, starting with a coin that's biased
toward telling them to answer randomly.

00:02:47.349 --> 00:02:57.870
And that — oddly this thing, randomized
response, is — fancy versions of it — what

00:02:57.870 --> 00:03:04.980
is being used by Apple now in iOS 10 and also
by Google for certain things in the Chrome

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:05.480
browser.

